  "worked_solution": "Recall that\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({f}^{′}(x))^{′}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $4\\in [3,6],$ where the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is represented by a straight line segment. So, ${f}^{″}(4)$ is just the slope of this segment.\n\nSince this segment passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,0)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(6,-4),$ we can compute its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{-4-0}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{″}(4)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$",
